A||||5||PBJ||Factory wing sag||||9||
Z||000000||PBJ||05-06-2001||05:33 PM||||Any known ways of reversing the sag of a factory wing on the black Fieros?  Any successful tips would be appreciated.||||reg||9
Z||000001||rogergarrison||05-06-2001||05:40 PM||||unless your willing to put another stansion in the center, Pretty sure you either have to replace it or live with it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||RNGCRSH||05-06-2001||07:01 PM||||I was talking with JetroGT this weekend, and the topic of a sagging wing came up. He told me you can take the wing off, and turn it upside down with the ends supported and a weight in the middle. Do This on a hot/sunny day and it should remove the sag. Then if you fill it with expanding foam it will hold its shape. <P><BR>Disclaimer: I have not tried this so do it at your own risk. <P>P.S. Before I bought a new wing, I would give it a try<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 05-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-07-2001||09:03 AM||||If it were me, I would cut an access out of the bottom, and glass in a piece of angle iron. It would probably need to be at least 1 1/2" x 1 1/2" but would last forever. I'd clamp the ends through the opening so as to bring the wing straight. The expanding foam is a good idea, but I wonder if it wouldn't eventually give out. Also, I don't know how thick the skin is on the wings, but that foam is pretty aggresive, and I've seen it push things right out of shape when it has no place to go.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phantom Rage||05-07-2001||01:31 PM||||I seem to remember someone on this forum mention stuffing a pillow under the center of the wing on a hot day.  I can’t really say if it works or not since I have never tried it myself.||||reg||7
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-07-2001||04:20 PM||||It took it 14 years to sag, dont think turning it over and leaving it in sun for a day is gonna do it. The idea of putting a bar thru it sounds workable, never took one apart to see if its hollow or what.||||reg||1
